  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "An array AAA consisting of 2⋅N2 \\cdot N2⋅N elements is said to be **good** if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n\n1≤Ai≤N1 \\le A_i \\le N1≤Ai​≤N for all 1≤i≤2⋅N1 \\le i \\le 2 \\cdot N1≤i≤2⋅N\nA2⋅i−1=A2⋅iA_{2 \\cdot i - 1} = A_{2 \\cdot i}A2⋅i−1​=A2⋅i​ for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N.\nA2⋅i≠A2⋅jA_{2 \\cdot i} \\ne A_{2 \\cdot j}A2⋅i​=A2⋅j​ for all 1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\le i &lt; j \\le N1≤i&lt;j≤N.\nFor example, some good arrays are [2,2,1,1][2, 2, 1, 1][2,2,1,1] and [2,2,1,1,3,3][2, 2, 1, 1, 3, 3][2,2,1,1,3,3].\n\nYou are given an array AAA which may not be good. You want to find the **minimum distance** to some good array BBB.\n\nThe distance between 222 arrays CCC and DDD (∣C∣=∣D∣|C| = |D|∣C∣=∣D∣) is the number of indices iii such that 1≤i≤∣C∣1 \\le i \\le |C|1≤i≤∣C∣ and Ci≠DiC_i \\ne D_iCi​=Di​.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains NNN.\nThe second line contains 2⋅N2 \\cdot N2⋅N integers - A1,A2,…,A2⋅NA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_{2 \\cdot N}A1​,A2​,…,A2⋅N​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum distance to a good array.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104",
      "2≤N≤2⋅1052 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^52≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤N1 \\le A_i \\le N1≤Ai​≤N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
